## Error Handling

All errors follow this consistent format:

```json
{
  "error": "Human-readable error message",
  "code": "ERROR_CODE",
  "status": 400,
  "details": {},
  "timestamp": "2026-05-11T12:00:00Z",
  "request_id": "req_abc123"
}
```

### Common HTTP Status Codes

| Code | Meaning | When It Happens |
|------|---------|-----------------|
| `200` | OK | Request succeeded |
| `201` | Created | Resource created successfully |
| `204` | No Content | Resource deleted successfully |
| `400` | Bad Request | Invalid request format |
| `401` | Unauthorized | Missing or invalid authentication |
| `403` | Forbidden | Valid auth but no permission |
| `404` | Not Found | Resource doesn't exist |
| `422` | Unprocessable Entity | Validation failed |
| `429` | Too Many Requests | Rate limit exceeded |
| `500` | Internal Server Error | Server-side error |

---

## Rate Limiting

API requests are rate-limited to prevent abuse:

| Tier | Limit |
|------|-------|
| Authenticated | 1000 requests / hour |
| Unauthenticated | 100 requests / hour |

Rate limit headers are included in every response:

```
X-RateLimit-Limit: 1000
X-RateLimit-Remaining: 999
X-RateLimit-Reset: 1715431200
```

When exceeded, you'll receive:

```json
{
  "error": "Rate limit exceeded",
  "code": "RATE_LIMIT_EXCEEDED",
  "status": 429,
  "retry_after": 3600
}
```

---

## Testing the API

### Using cURL

```bash
# Health check
curl http://localhost:8080/

# Get all users (with auth)
curl -H "Authorization: Bearer <token>" http://localhost:8080/api/users

# Create a user
curl -X POST http://localhost:8080/api/users   -H "Content-Type: application/json"   -d '{"name":"Test","email":"test@example.com","password":"password123"}'
```

### Using Postman / Insomnia

1. Import the collection: `postman_collection.json` (if available)
2. Set environment variable `base_url` to `http://localhost:8080`
3. Set `auth_token` after logging in

### Using the Frontend

The Next.js frontend at `http://localhost:3000` consumes these APIs automatically. Check the browser's Network tab to see live API calls.

---

## Data Persistence

The backend uses two persistent volumes mounted from the host:

| Volume | Container Path | Description |
|--------|---------------|-------------|
| `./backend/database` | `/app/database` | SQLite / database files |
| `./backend/uploads` | `/app/uploads` | Uploaded files (avatars, images, etc.) |

> **Note:** These folders are created automatically on first run and persist across container restarts.

